#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --frozen python
"""scripts/impacted-tests — the ONE diff -> tests selector.

Used by every lane that asks "which tests does this change affect":
``scripts/ship --checked`` (the qland middle rung), ``scripts/test
--selected``, and the measured tiers keep a ``BASE HEAD`` mode for the
deferred release lane — release gating on the impacted set is deferred
until the selector has a track record (the full suite gates releases).
There is deliberately no second selector — a coupling only has to be
recorded once here.

Input modes:

  scripts/impacted-tests BASE [HEAD]   committed diff (release lane)
  scripts/impacted-tests               worktree diff: merge-base..HEAD + dirty
  scripts/impacted-tests --files a b   explicit changed paths (repo-relative)
  scripts/impacted-tests --stdin       changed paths on stdin (ship --checked)

Output: BASE mode prints ``file<TAB>reason`` (or ``FULL``); the worktree
family prints line 1 ``testmon=0|1`` then one pytest target per line (or
``FULL`` alone) — the wire protocol ``scripts/select-tests`` had, so
``ship --checked`` / ``test --selected`` parse it unchanged. ``testmon=1``
means this worktree has a ``.testmondata`` map AND Python code is in the
affected set — the caller should additionally run ``pytest --testmon``.

Evidence, strongest first:

  measured   the per-test-context coverage DB (``.coverage`` + the
             ``.coverage.precis.json`` manifest that ``scripts/test
             --coverage`` or the nightly coverage job leave). Used only
             when the DB is fresh (manifest ``created_at`` within
             MAX_DB_AGE_HOURS and not older than the base commit) AND a
             changed file's manifest hash matches its BASE blob —
             coverage speaks base-side line numbers. Each diff hunk is
             widened to its enclosing base-side symbol before lookup.
  static     the python index's call graph + imports: for each changed
             .py module, ``discover_tests`` per changed symbol; test
             modules that import the changed module; test files named
             after it (``test_<stem>``); test sources that name the
             changed path/basename (covers scripts, fixtures, prompts,
             skills — anything a test reads by name).
  contract   CONTRACT — the fast repo-invariant tests that always run:
             audited tables, closed registries, ratchets, doc/secret/vocab
             scans. They assert over the whole tree, so a data or config
             change can red them with no import edge at all.
  corpus     PATH_MAP — explicit glob -> target couplings the scans cannot
             see (handler mode/arg vocab vs the audited parity table,
             reviewed-blob pins, migrations, skill corpus) — plus resolved
             corpus refs: every test (and src module) is scanned for the
             repo paths it reads (string literals, ``Path(__file__)``-
             anchored joins, ``importlib.resources.files(<pkg>)``, dir
             enumerations), and a changed path selects every test whose
             refs cover it.
  always     never narrowed away: a changed test file is itself;
             ``*/migrations/*.sql`` selects the migration/schema set;
             docs-only diffs select the doc-hygiene set; a non-doc file
             with zero other evidence adds the smoke set (unsure widens).
  full       pyproject/uv.lock/conftest/scripts/test/docker/workflow
             changes run the WHOLE suite — those change the harness
             itself, so no selection is honest. Prints ``FULL``.

Usage:
  scripts/impacted-tests BASE HEAD --list  bare paths for xargs/pytest
  scripts/impacted-tests --smoke           the fixed smoke set
  scripts/impacted-tests --migrations      the migration/schema set
  scripts/impacted-tests BASE HEAD --json  machine-readable selection
  scripts/impacted-tests --list-contract   the CONTRACT + PATH_MAP tables
  --db PATH    coverage DB (or dir holding one); default: ./.coverage
               then $PRECIS_COVERAGE_DB_DIR/.coverage
  --fetch-db   download the latest nightly coverage artifact into the
               cache dir (needs gh) — off by default

Static fan-out runs on the indexed working tree (same documented
limitation as the python kind's impact view); at gate time the worktree
IS the head, so this costs nothing.
"""

